BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:talks-273701@cambridge-events
DTSTAMP:20261118T132725Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261117T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261117T140000
SUMMARY:Gendered Land Use and Agency in the Brazilian Amazon and Beyond
LOCATION:Small Lecture Theatre\, Department of Geography
URL:https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273701/
CATEGORIES:Politics,Policy
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Leonie Hodel\, University of Cambridge\nPolitical Ecol
 ogy Group Seminar Series\nTopics: Politics\, Policy\nDetails: https://talk
 s.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273701/\nLand system science has improved our under
 standing of how deforestation traces to markets\, commodity chains\, and p
 olicy spillovers. Yet the socio-cultural relations through which these dri
 vers become decisions on the ground\, including values\, worldviews and ge
 nder\, are routinely reduced to a residual “other” category. This talk
  asks what changes when gender is treated not as context but as constituti
 ve of land-use change. \n\n Drawing on fieldwork in Acre\, a deforestation
  frontier shaped by state-led colonisation\, rubber-tapper and Indigenous 
 struggles\, and the rise of cattle ranching\, I show how pasture expansion
  rests on a gendered order. Ranching is coded as masculine\, productive wo
 rk\, and the ideal of the “clean” pasture over “messy” forest is b
 ound up with ideas of the successful man and household. Women’s labour i
 s largely absent from accounts of this expansion. Even where women are 
END:VEVENT
